WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
Every day, doctors make their
rounds, patient by patient.

2
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.240
Students need those reps too, but
practice patients are hard to find.

3
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.340
Rounds puts one on call, anytime.

4
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.640
Talk to them out loud, then
see exactly what you missed.

5
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.340
My name's Ahmad Higazy, and I'm a fourth-year
medical student at the Geisinger Commonwealth

6
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:20.280
School of Medicine.

7
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.700
Working alongside a team of software
engineers and biochemists, we've been

8
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:25.380
tackling a problem

9
00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.000
that I faced in my own medical education
experience, and that's providing realistic

10
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.800
clinical simulation for medical students in
the preclinical phase of their curriculum.

11
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.520
Now, the canonical way it's done with
standardized patients is cost-intensive

12
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:40.700
and not flexible

13
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:45.440
enough to accommodate the necessary experience
students need before entering the clinical

14
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:45.860
space.

15
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:50.000
And that's why we built Rounds, which is an
immersive clinical experience that can be

16
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.000
built in minutes.

17
00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:52.040
Let's dive right in.

18
00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.360
So, Rounds was built keeping in mind
both the educator and the learner.

19
00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:00.140
On the educator side of things,
we've streamlined case creation.

20
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.340
So here you can tap to describe the case.

21
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:08.800
Let's say we want a case for a third-year
medical student involving Legionella pneumonia

22
00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:15.200
in a middle-aged male that takes
lisinopril for their hypertension.

23
00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:20.920
And when the student elicits their travel
history, the patient will mention that they've

24
00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:26.140
recently been to a conference in Philadelphia
where they believe they caught a cold due

25
00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:29.460
to the freezing cold air
conditioning units there.

26
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:33.560
And from there, we have this
populated and then we build the case.

27
00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.880
So after submitting your description of the
case, a state-of-the-art large language model

28
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.540
interprets it and fills in
the rest of the picture.

29
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.980
You can go back and forth here and continuously
refine the picture until it's just how you

30
00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:45.340
like it.

31
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:51.780
So following this, you're greeted with a
page that displays a wide variety of options

32
00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.860
that faculty can use to customize
the experience for students.

33
00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.660
Once you're ready to
publish, you hit complete.

34
00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.780
So now that the case has been built, let's
go and see what a student experiences.

35
00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.600
Now students are greeted with a dashboard
that includes their recent performance on

36
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.260
cases and cases left to do,
including the case we just made.

37
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.540
Now upon opening a case, a wide
variety of information is displayed.

38
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.600
Once you're ready to begin the call,
you can hit connect microphone.

39
00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.000
What's been bothering you most?

40
00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:20.200
My wife made me come in.

41
00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.700
I've had a fever and this cough.

42
00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:26.420
And now just walking to the
kitchen leaves me out of breath.

43
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:28.920
Have you been around anyone who's been ill?

44
00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.640
Maybe at this work conference in Philadelphia.

45
00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.940
The AC was blasting the whole time.

46
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.440
I figured I'd picked up a cold there.

47
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.180
Your fever, cough, and worsening breathing
makes me concerned about pneumonia,

48
00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:43.940
an infection in the lungs.

49
00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:45.240
Pneumonia?

50
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.460
Could I have caught that at the hotel?

51
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.700
The combined depth across our team's
disciplines allowed us to build something

52
00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:55.200
more than just a virtual patient
or a simulated environment.

53
00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.500
At the center, we built a critic,

54
00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:00.620
a personalized model of each
student's clinical decision making.

55
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.040
Our virtual patient used an LLM
which defined clinical personas and

56
00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:09.760
behavioral priors that dynamically
respond to each of the student's strong

57
00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.680
suits and weak points.

58
00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.040
Meanwhile, our critic combines TF-IDF

59
00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.520
logistic regression to classify
actions with an LLM evaluator to

60
00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:20.820
assess clinical performance.

61
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.940
These evaluations create interpretable
profiles that drive evidence-based debriefs.

62
00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:29.400
We explored every dataset provided
and used about 70% of it.

63
00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:35.080
Almost 7,000 labeled communication turns
and nearly 300 clinical interviews.

64
00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.340
And we developed 900 action
classification examples.

65
00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:42.380
Comparing our simulated learning to
real studies on active scenarios,

66
00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.720
our next target is a 25%
reduction in time to competency.

67
00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:51.540
That's before counting that students
can practice here any time,

68
00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.760
far more often than an actor
could ever be scheduled.

69
00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.100
Although our aim was ultimately to build
fast, we knew that we had to do it right.

70
00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.840
From the very beginning, we knew that we
were going to use the industry standard

71
00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:07.800
of AWS services, going from reliable
databases to serverless compute,

72
00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:12.280
frontier large language models using
Bedrock, transcription tools using

73
00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:16.520
HealthScribe, which many may be familiar
with, and user authentication using Cognito.

74
00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:19.240
So that means that we might not have
to memorize all these passwords,

75
00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:23.900
using things such as OIDC to integrate
into your already existing workflows.

76
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.940
In addition to AWS, we also made
sure to use specialized speech and

77
00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:32.640
video generation AI models using ElevenLabs
and Tavus, utilizing state-of-the-art

78
00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.820
models to provide unique and memorable
value to our next generation of

79
00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:36.440
medical professionals.
